# rules: java-credential-is-a-literal
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-798.java
# from: CWE-798 (Use of Hard-coded Credentials), CWE-259
#
# A password written into a `getConnection` call is in version control, in the
# build artifact, and readable out of the jar with `strings`. It cannot be
# rotated without a release, everyone who has ever cloned the repository still
# knows it, and it is usually the same password on staging as on production,
# because writing it down is what made that convenient.
#
# The position is what makes this checkable without guessing. `getConnection`
# takes the password third and nothing else; `setPassword` and
# `setProperty("password", ...)` say what they are. So the rule asks about an
# argument in a credential position and does not go looking for names that
# sound like secrets, which is the version of this rule that reports every
# constant in a program.
#
# Two literals are excluded and both exclusions matter more than they look:
#
#   - the empty string. A local H2 or SQLite database with no password is the
#     usual reason for `getConnection(url, "sa", "")`, and it is not a secret
#     that leaked.
#   - `${...}` and `#{...}`. That is a Spring or Ant placeholder naming a
#     value that lives somewhere else, which is the fix. Reporting it would
#     train people to skip this rule on exactly the codebases that got it
#     right.
#   - the literal that is the *name* of the thing rather than a value of it:
#     `setPassword("password")`, `("pwd2")`, `("pass")`, `("foo")`. Nobody's
#     production password is the string `password`; these are what a test
#     writes when it needs an argument. Spring's own suite has twenty-eight of
#     them and twenty-three are the single word `password`, which is the count
#     that made this exclusion necessary rather than tidy.
#
# `changeit` is deliberately *not* excluded. It is the default password of
# every Java keystore, and finding it left in is the point.
#
# The constant field is the second reading, and it is the spelling people
# actually write:
#
#	private static final String DB_PASSWORD = "s3cr3t-prod-pw";
#
# It used to be unreachable - a `$T $F = $E;` pattern was read as a local and
# nothing else - and this rule's header used to say so. xen0bit/pwrq#51 taught
# the matcher that a declaration has three names in Java, so the shape is now
# a pattern like any other.
#
# It has to ask a different question from the positional reading, though, and
# a weaker one: a field has no argument position to sit in, so the only thing
# left to go on is its name. That is the version of this rule that reports
# every constant in a program, so it is held to four tests at once.
#
# The first is position, and it is the one that does the most work. A
# declaration pattern reads a local as readily as a field, and a local called
# `password` holding a literal is test data - Spring's own suite has four of
# them, `String password = "spring"` among them, and every one is a value in
# flight rather than a credential baked into the program. `outside` the method
# declarations is how the rule says "a field": what is left is declared on the
# class, which is what shipping a credential looks like.
#
# The other three are that the name is a credential word, that the value is a
# literal, and that the value is not one of
# the things a literal in a credential-shaped constant usually is:
#
#   - the name of an environment variable or a property. `SCREAMING_SNAKE` and
#     `dotted.lower` are both names of somewhere the value lives, which is the
#     fix rather than the bug, and the Go pass in this corpus learned that the
#     expensive way with `const TokenEnv = "PWRQ_MCP_TOKEN"`.
#   - a name for the field rather than a value for it: a constant called
#     `PASSWORD_PARAM` whose value is `"password"` is a form field's name.
#   - an HTTP header. Guava's `ACCESS_CONTROL_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS =
#     "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials"` is a credential-shaped name holding a
#     header's name, and the header is spelled in Title-Case segments joined by
#     hyphens, which nothing anybody chose as a password looks like.
#
# and the name itself must not be one of those either, which is what the
# `_PARAM`/`_FIELD`/`_HEADER` suffixes are doing in the second regex.

["DriverManager.getConnection($_, $_, $PW)",
 "$DS.setPassword($PW)", "$DS.setCredentials($PW)",
 "new UsernamePasswordCredentials($_, $PW)",
 "new PasswordAuthentication($_, $PW)",
 "new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken($_, $PW)",
 "new BasicAWSCredentials($_, $PW)"] as $positional
| ["$P.setProperty($K, $PW)", "$P.put($K, $PW)"] as $keyed
| "^\"[^\"]" as $literal
| "^\"\\$\\{|^\"#\\{|^\"%\\(|^\"<" as $placeholder
| ( "(?i)^\"(pass(word)?|passwd|pwd|pw|secret|credential|token|key|"
    + "user(name)?|admin|root|foo|bar|baz|qux|test|example|sample|dummy|"
    + "placeholder|value|string|none|null|todo|x+|\\*+|\\.+)[0-9]?\"" ) as $theWordItself
| "(?i)^\"(pass|password|passwd|pwd|secret|credential|api[._-]?key|auth[._-]?token)\"$" as $credentialKey
| ["$T $PWF = $PW;"] as $constants
| ["$RT $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| ( "(?i)passw(or)?d|passwd|pwd|secret|api[_.-]?key|access[_.-]?token|"
    + "auth[_.-]?token|credential|private[_.-]?key|client[_.-]?secret" ) as $credentialName
| ( "(?i)(param|field|header|attribute|column|property|prop|prompt|label|"
    + "encoder|policy|pattern|regex|_?name|key_?name|env)$" ) as $namesTheField
| ( "^\"[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*\"$|^\"[a-z0-9]+(\\.[a-z0-9]+)+\"$|"
    + "^\"[A-Z][a-z0-9]*(-[A-Z][a-z0-9]*)+\"$" ) as $namesSomewhereElse
| scan_ast("*.java"; $positional + $keyed + $constants + $methods) as $all
| ( ($all | of($positional))
    + ($all | of($keyed) | where_capture("K"; $credentialKey))
    + ($all | of($constants)
       | outside($all | of($methods))
       | where_capture("PWF"; $credentialName)
       | where_capture_not("PWF"; $namesTheField)
       | where_capture_not("PW"; $namesSomewhereElse)) )
| where_capture("PW"; $literal)
| where_capture_not("PW"; $placeholder)
| where_capture_not("PW"; $theWordItself)
| focus("PW")
| finding("java-credential-is-a-literal";
    "this password is written into the program, so it is in version control "
    + "and in the jar, and changing it means shipping a release")
| report
